---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { Counter } from "../components/Counter";

/**
 * Two islands on one page, and a lot of static content between them.
 *
 * Each island hydrates independently, with its own React root. They do not
 * share state and cannot, which is the constraint that decides whether Astro
 * suits an app: two counters on a page is fine, a basket whose contents three
 * separate islands need is not.
 *
 * The usual answers when islands do need to share something: put the state in
 * the URL, in a small store outside React (nanostores is the common choice),
 * or merge the islands into one.
 */
---

<Base title="Islands">
  <main>
    <h1>Islands</h1>

    <p>
      Two React components with <code>client:load</code>, separated by static HTML. Each is its
      own React root, hydrated separately.
    </p>

    <Counter label="First island" testId="first" client:load />

    <div class="card">
      <h2>Static content between them</h2>
      <p>
        This heading and paragraph are plain HTML. They cost nothing, they are in the page before
        any JavaScript runs, and no framework is involved in rendering them.
      </p>
    </div>

    <Counter label="Second island" testId="second" client:load />

    <h2>They cannot share state</h2>
    <p>
      Increment one and the other does not move. Each island is a separate React root with a
      separate tree, so there is no context, no store and no parent between them. That is the
      constraint to weigh before choosing Astro for something app-shaped.
    </p>

    <p class="note">
      When islands genuinely need shared state: put it in the URL, put it in a framework-agnostic
      store outside React, or make them one island.
    </p>
  </main>
</Base>
